`2013-12-31`.\nL67: \nL68: * * *\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Submission date (most recent) [Input] Submission date (original) [Input] Announcement date\nL71: \nL72: You may filter on either submission date or announcement date. Note that announcement date supports only year and month granularity.\nL73: [Input] Show abstracts [Input] Hide abstracts\nL74: \nL75: [Select]\nL76: \nL77: results per page\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Include older versions of papers\nL80: \nL81: [Button: Search] [Input]\nL82: #### Searching by Author Name\nL83:   * Using the Author(s) field produces best results for author name searches.\nL84:   * For the most precise name search, follow surname(s), forename(s) or surname(s), initial(s) pattern: example Hawking, S or Hawking, Stephen\nL85:   * For best results on multiple author names, separate individuals with a ; (semicolon). Example: Jin, D S; Ye, J\nL86:   * Author names enclosed in quotes will return only exact matches. For example, \"Stephen Hawking\" will not return matches for Stephen W. Hawking.\nL87:   * Diacritic character variants are automatically searched in the Author(s) field.\nL88:   * Queries with no punctuation will treat each term independently.\nL89: #### Searching by subcategory\nL90: \nL91:   * To search within a subcategory select All fields.\nL92:   * A subcategory search can be combined with an author or keyword search by clicking on add another term in advanced search.\nL93: ## Tips\nL94: \nL95: Wildcards:\nL96: \nL97:   * Use ? to replace a single character or * to replace any number of characters.\nL98:   * Can be used in any field, but not in the first character position. See Journal References tips for exceptions.\nL99: \nL100: Expressions:\nL101: \nL102:   * TeX expressions can be searched, enclosed in single $ characters.\nL103: \nL104: Phrases:\nL105: \nL106:   * Enclose phrases in double quotes for exact matches in title, abstract, and comments.\nL107: \nL108: Dates:\nL109:   * Sorting by announcement date will use the year and month the original version (v1) of the paper was announced.\nL110:   * Sorting by submission date will use the year, month and day the latest version of the paper was submitted.\nL111: \nL112: Journal References:\nL113:   * If a journal reference search contains a wildcard, matches will be made using wildcard matching as expected. For example, math* will match math, maths, mathematics.\nL114:   * If a journal reference search does not contain a wildcard, only exact phrases entered will be matched. For example, math would match math or math and science but not maths or mathematics.\nL115:   * All journal reference searches that do not contain a wildcard are literal searches: a search for Physica A will match all papers with journal references containing Physica A, but a search for Physica A, 245 (1997) 181 will only return the paper with journal reference Physica A, 245 (1997) 181.\nL116: cite6†Search v0.5.6 released 2020-02-24†github.com L117: \nL118: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite8†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL119: \nL120: cite9†About†info.arxiv.org · cite10†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite11†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite12†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite13†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite14†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite15†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite16†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L121: \nL122: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Good is Post-Hoc Watermarking With Language Model Rephrasing? (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16904v1)\nciteturn6469view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6467view0\",\"lineno\":163}); Total lines: 729\nL154: This embeds a statistical signal into the generated text that is imperceptible to readers but detectable by the detector. We provide a comprehensive description of the watermarking schemes evaluated in this work in cite46†subsection A.2 .\nL155: #### Detection and statistical test.\nL156: Detection recomputes the pseudorandom function for each group of tokens and aggregates the results into a test statistic. Under the null hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$ “not watermarked with this specific scheme”, the statistic should follow a known distribution, yielding a $p$-value for the probability of observing such a score or a bigger score by chance.\nL157: Under the alternative hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{1}$ “watermarked text with this specific scheme”, the statistic should deviate significantly enough and lead to low $p$-values. We flag text as watermarked if the $p$-value is below a detection threshold $\\alpha$, which controls the false positive rate (FPR).\nL158: For instance, if $K$ (the test statistic here) is the observed number of green tokens out of $N$ scored tokens and that this statistic follows under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$ a Binomial distribution with parameters $N$ and $\\gamma$ (expected green ratio, typically $0.5$), then $p\\text{-value}=\\mathbb{P}\\left(X\\geq K\\mid X\\sim\\text{Binomial}(N,\\gamma)\\right)$.\nL159: As an example, if we observe $K=65$ green tokens out of $N=100$ with $\\gamma=0.5$, the $p$-value is approximately $10^{-3}$; to flag this text as watermarked, we would need a FPR of $\\alpha\\geq 10^{-3}$.\nL160: An important practical consideration is deduplication: because the pseudorandom function depends on the preceding $k$ tokens, repeated n-grams generate identical hashes. This violates the statistical independence of the scores used when assuming the distribution of the test statistic under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$.\nL161: Aggregating scores only over unique watermark windows within the text (cite77†Kirchenbauer et al., 2023a ; cite101†Fernandez et al., 2023 ) is a good way to mitigate this issue and to ensure valid $p$-values. But deduplication alone may not guarantee valid statistical tests because natural language inherently favors certain n-grams over others. This bias can cause $p$-values to be artificially low or high even for unwatermarked text.\nL162: To address this, we ensure that under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$, $p$-values are approximately uniform on $U(0,1)$. In practice, we test many candidate secret keys and select one for which the empirical $p$-value distribution on unwatermarked text is close to uniform. We provide more details in cite45†subsection A.1 .\nL163: ### 3.2 Compute-Driven Flexibility\nL164: Operating in a post-hoc setting offers more flexibility than in-generation watermarks, which must comply with live-serving constraints such as latency, memory, and decoding speed (cite78†Dathathri et al., 2024 ). Here, we can use large or small models, run higher temperatures or beam search instead of simple sampling, and even employ multi-candidate selection like WaterMax (cite116†Giboulot and Furon, 2024 ), as a function of available compute and quality targets.\nL165: We detail the specific higher-compute decoding (and detecting) methods we test below.\nL166: #### Beam search for watermarking.\nL167: We use a beam search approach to improve the quality-detectability trade-off. Specifically, we maintain $B$ candidate sequences and expands each beam with $V$ candidates sampled from the watermarked probability distribution $\\mathbf{p}_{\\text{wm}}(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$, which is derived by applying the watermarking scheme (e.g., Maryland, SynthID, DIPMark, MorphMark) to the base model’s logits. To select the top-$B$ beams at each step, we score candidates using log-probability under a reference distribution.\nL168: We explore two scoring variants: unbiased scoring uses the original model probabilities $\\mathbf{p}_{\\text{orig}}(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$ to favor sequences with lower perplexity relative to the base model, prioritizing text quality; biased scoring uses the watermarked probabilities $\\mathbf{p}_{\\text{wm}}(\\cdot\\mid x_{<t})$ to favor sequences that are most probable under the watermarked distribution, potentially yielding stronger watermark signals.\nL169: We note that the latter was also explored for generation-time watermarking in (cite120†Kirchenbauer et al., 2023b ). For candidate generation, we consider both deterministic beam search (selecting top-$V$ tokens by $\\mathbf{p}_{\\text{wm}}$) and stochastic beam search (sampling $V$ tokens from $\\mathbf{p}_{\\text{wm}}$).\nL170: #### Multi-candidate selection with Wateremax (cite116†Giboulot and Furon, 2024 )\nL171: Rephrasing is done chunk by chunk of $L$ tokens. For each chunk, we generate $m$ candidates $\\{\\tilde{y}^{(1)},\\dots,\\tilde{y}^{(m)}\\}$ without applying any logit bias. We then select the candidate that naturally maximizes the watermark score: $y^{*}=\\arg\\max_{y}\\text{Score}_{\\text{wm}}(y)$. This method is “distortion-free” regarding the sampling distribution but is typically infeasible in standard API usage due to cost (generating $L$ times more tokens per chunk).\nL172: #### Entropy-aware detection.\nL173: At detection time, we compute the entropy $H_{t}$ of the model’s predicted distribution at each token position $t$ in the watermarked text $H_{t}=-\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{t}(v)\\log p_{t}(v)$ where $p_{t}(v)$ is the model probability for token $v$ given the prefix up to $t-1$ in the watermarked text. We then apply an entropy filter: a token is included in the watermark score only if $H_{t}$ exceeds a chosen threshold $\\tau$, similar to what is done in cite107†Lee et al. (2023) for code.\nL174: This focuses detection on high-entropy positions, where watermarking is more effective, and ignores low-entropy tokens. All entropy computations are performed on the watermarked text, as the original text is not available at detection time. As a result, these entropy values differ from those at generation-time, when the model was conditioned.\nL175: ## 4 Experiments\nL176: We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of post-hoc watermarking to assess: (1) detection power (the ability to reliably identify watermarked text, measured through $p$-values) and (2) fidelity (the preservation of meaning and quality through paraphrasing). In cite22†subsection 4.1 , we detail the experimental setup, including datasets, models, watermarking schemes, decoding strategies, and evaluation metrics.\nL177: In cite31†subsection 4.2 , we compare watermarking methods on the quality-detectability Pareto frontier, finding that Gumbel-max dominates under standard sampling. In cite32†subsection 4.3 , we show that larger models preserve semantics better, but small/mid-size models offer sweet spots, with stronger watermark signals due to higher entropy. In cite33†subsection 4.4 , we demonstrate that beam search improves the Pareto frontier for all applicable methods.\nL178: In cite34†subsection 4.5 , we evaluate entropy-aware detection and find only modest gains. In cite36†subsection 4.6 , we investigate post-hoc watermarking on code, revealing that correctness constraints limit detectability. In cite40†subsection 4.7 , we evaluate cross-lingual robustness and, in cite41†subsection 4.8 , the impact of chunking on long documents.\nL179: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL180: #### Datasets.\nL181: \nL182: We evaluate on three diverse corpora to capture different text characteristics and use cases:\nL183: \nL184:   * •\nL185: \nL186: Books: Passages in English from the Gutenberg dataset (cite121†Project Gutenberg, 2025 ), containing books. These represent longer-form, literary text with complex sentence structures.\nL187: \nL188:   * •\nL189: \nL190: Wikipedia: Lead paragraphs from 500 randomly sampled Wikipedia articles. These represent factual, encyclopedic text with a formal writing style and different languages.\nL191: \nL192:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPixel Seal: Adversarial-only training for invisible image and video watermarking (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16874v1)\nciteturn6469view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6467view1\",\"lineno\":110}); Total lines: 408\nL98: Losses. The post-hoc image and video watermarking training commonly use a combination of perceptual, adversarial, and message losses. The perceptual loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{perc}}$ ensures the watermarked image $x_{w}$ produced by the watermark embedder is close to the original input image $x$.\nL99: The exact implementation of the loss varies significantly in the literature, ranging from simple mean squared error (MSE) to more complex ones, such as LPIPS (cite80†Zhang et al., 2018 ), focal frequency (cite81†Jiang et al., 2021 ), or Watson perceptual models (cite82†Czolbe et al., 2020 ). To improve the imperceptibility of the embedded watermark, many works rely on the adversarial loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}$ often formulated as $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}=-D(x_{w})$.\nL100: It maximizes the likelihood that a watermarked image $x_{w}$ is classified as non-watermarked by the discriminator network $D$. The discriminator itself is jointly trained using a dual-hinge loss (cite83†Lim et al., 2017 ) to distinguish between original and watermarked images. Lastly, to hide a binary message $m$ in the watermarked image, the binary cross-entropy loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}$ is applied to the extractor outputs $\\tilde{m}$:\nL101:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}(m,\\tilde{m})=-\\frac{1}{n_{\\text{bits}}}\\sum_{k=1}^{n_{\\text{bits}}}m_{k}\\log(\\tilde{m}_{k})+(1-m_{k})\\log(1-\\tilde{m}_{k}).$$  |  | (3)\nL102: \nL103: The loss is backpropagated through both the extractor and the embedder to guide them to correctly embed and read the hidden message in the watermark. The final loss function used for the training is the weighted combination of the three losses:\nL104:  | $$\\mathcal{L}=\\lambda_{\\text{perc}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{perc}}+\\lambda_{\\text{adv}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}+\\lambda_{\\text{msg}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}.$$  |  | (4)\nL105: Augmentations. To ensure the embedded message in the watermarked image can be read even if a user modifies the image, during training, the watermarked images are randomly transformed before being passed to the extractor. Common transformations include geometric changes (e.g., cropping, resizing, and rotation), valuemetric adjustments (e.g., brightness and contrast), and compression (e.g., JPEG). Some transformations, such as the JPEG compression, are not differentiable.\nL106: In such a case, similarly to cite84†Zhang et al. (2021) , this work uses a straight-through estimator that approximates the gradient with the identity function:\nL107:  | $$\\tilde{x}_{w}=x_{w}+\\text{nograd}(T(x_{w})-x_{w}),$$  |  | (5)\nL108: \nL109: where $T$ is the non-differentiable transformation. Other works approximate non-differentiable transformations with a trained neural network (cite70†Luo et al., 2023 ; cite71†Shen et al., 2023 ).\nL110: ## 4 Invisible and robust image watermarking\nL111: Image watermarking presents several key challenges. First, the watermark must be invisible to humans; any visible artifacts, especially in flat regions such as the sky, are highly distracting to the viewer. Second, the watermark must be detectable even if the watermarked image is altered by various user edits, such as cropping or Instagram filters. Training such a robust model is highly sensitive to exact hyperparameter values, and multiple random initializations can yield significantly different outcomes.\nL112: Lastly, watermarking must be fast and imperceptible even for very high-resolution images. A common inference-time interpolation technique for high-resolution watermarking introduces distracting artifacts that are easily detectable by the human eye.\nL113: To address these challenges, we introduce the following contributions. First, to produce invisible watermarks, we introduce adversarial-only training without any perceptual losses (Section cite12†4.1 ). Second, to make the model robust and the training stable and repeatable, we introduce a three-stage training schedule in which we first achieve visible but very robust watermarks that are made invisible later during training (Section cite13†4.2 ).\nL114: Third, to allow for practical high-resolution watermarking, we propose training-time inference simulation to eliminate upscaling artifacts and increase model’s robustness (Section cite14†4.3 ). Lastly, we introduce temporal watermark pooling—a technique for efficiently adapting image watermarking models to video (Section cite15†4.4 ).\nL115: cite85†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Our training setup. Pixel Seal is trained using only adversarial and message loss, which results in highly imperceptible watermarks. The message loss is backpropagated through the simulated attack (image augmentation) to ensure the embedded watermarks are robust to common user edits.\nL116: ### 4.1 Adversarial-only loss function\nL117: Ensuring that a watermark remains invisible in the content is the primary challenge in post-hoc watermarking research. For example, cite50†Bui et al. (2023) utilize four different losses specifically designed to make watermarks invisible. However, many such loss functions have modes in which the loss value is small, but the watermark is very visible to humans. Therefore, making the watermark invisible requires careful hyperparameter tuning without any guarantees of success.\nL118: And even with carefully tuned hyperparameters, watermarks often remain visible in flat areas as waves (cite50†Bui et al., 2023 ) or blobs (cite78†Fernandez et al., 2024 ).\nL119: In the attempt to solve the imperceptibility issue, in contrast to previous work, as shown in Figure cite86†2 , we remove all perceptual losses and train the watermarking model using only the cross-entropy message loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{msg}}$ and an adversarial loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}(x,x_{w})$:\nL120: \nL121:  | $$\\frac{1}{2}\\nabla_{\\theta_{D}}\\Bigl[\\text{ReLU}\\bigl(1-D(x)\\bigr)+\\text{ReLU}\\bigl(1+D(x_{w})\\bigr)\\Bigr]-\\lambda_{adv}\\nabla_{\\theta_{\\text{Emb}}}D(x_{w})$$  |  | (6)\nL122: where $x$ and $x_{w}$ are the original and watermarked images, $D$ is a patch-based discriminator network, $\\theta_{D}$ its parameters, and $\\theta_{\\text{Emb}}$ are the parameters of the watermark embedder. The architecture of the discriminator affects how effectively it can remove visible artifacts. The more effective the discriminator is in removing visible artifacts, the less robust the final watermark is to various content modifications.\nL123: In this work, we chose the patch-based discriminator of cite26†Rombach et al. (2022) as it provided a good balance.\nL124: To control the imperceptibility of the watermark, we introduce the watermark boosting technique. The watermark boosting artificially amplifies the artifacts that are present in a watermarked image, making them easier for the discriminator to be detected and removed. In detail, the boosted watermarked image $\\hat{x}_{w}$, computed as $\\hat{x}_{w}=x+\\beta(x_{w}-x)$, replaces the regular watermarked image $x_{w}$ in Equation (cite87†6 ).\nL125: Setting $\\beta>1$ ensures greater imperceptibility of the watermark, while $\\beta<1$ allows for very robust watermarks.\nL126: While the adversarial loss ensures the watermarked image does not contain any noticeable unnatural artifacts, the watermarked image can still significantly deviate from the original reference image.\nL127: This is a lesser issue for watermarking of AI-generated content, where users are not provided with the original reference image, but it can be a major challenge for other provenance applications (cite30†Castro, 2025 ), such as professional photography devices, where the watermark must be minimal (ideally comparable to camera noise), so as not to alter the user’s intent.\nL128: To restrict the allowed deviation the watermark $w$ can introduce to the original content, we reduce the watermark magnitude by a global scaling factor $\\alpha$ and a local Just Noticeable Difference (cite88†Zhang et al., 2008 ) attenuation map as done by cite55†Sander et al. (2025) .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning to Watermark in the Latent Space of Generative Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.16140v1)\nciteturn6469view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6467view2\",\"lineno\":135}); Total lines: 589\nL124: We train the watermark embedder $W_{\\theta}$ and watermark extractor $E_{\\theta}$ using a combination of watermark extraction loss and discriminator loss. The watermarking extraction loss ensures that the watermark message $m$ can be accurately extracted from the watermarked image $x_{w}$. The discriminator loss, applied in the pixel space, tries to enforce that the watermarked image $x_{w}$ is indistinguishable from real images.\nL125: For the quantized latents, we observe that the perturbations in the latent space introduced by the embedder can result in semantic modifications to the image. Therefore, we do not use reconstruction losses or perceptual losses for any of the post-hoc watermark embedder and only constrain the watermark with the watermarking strength $\\epsilon$ and the discriminator loss. The overall loss function is given by:\nL126:  | $$\\mathcal{L}=\\lambda_{w}\\mathcal{L}_{w}(x_{w},m)+\\lambda_{\\text{disc}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{disc}}(x,x_{w}).$$  |  | (1)\nL127: ### 4.2 Distillation for In-Model Watermarking\nL128: \nL129: We may optionally distill the latent watermarker into the generative model (diffusion or autoregressive transformer) by watermarking the latents of the training images and fine-tuning on this watermarked data.\nL130: Formally, given latents $z$ and a fixed message $m$ used across training images, we generate continuous or discrete watermarked latents $z_{w}=z+\\epsilon W_{\\theta}(z,m)$ or $z_{w}=Q(z+\\epsilon W_{\\theta}(z,m))$ using the trained embedder $W_{\\theta}$. The generative model is then fine-tuned to reconstruct or predict $z_{w}$ instead of $z$, encouraging it to produce watermarked latents during the generation process. The loss function for distillation in the generative model reads:\nL131:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{gen}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{recon}}(G_{\\phi}(x),z_{w}),$$  |  | (2)\nL132: \nL133: where $G_{\\phi}$ denotes the generative model (diffusion or autoregressive), $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{recon}}$ is the reconstruction loss (e.g., MSE or cross-entropy, resp.), and $x$ represents the input (noisy latent or token sequence, resp.). After distillation, the generative model embeds the watermark during the generation phase, eliminating the need for a separate post-hoc step.\nL134: This approach can also be applied to the latent decoder $\\mathcal{D}$ by training it to reconstruct watermarked images from non-watermarked latents, i.e., by training $\\mathcal{D}(z)$ to be close to $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{o}}(z_{w})$, where $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{o}}$ is the original decoder and $z_{w}$ the watermarked latent.\nL135: We optimize a combination of a reconstruction loss $\\mathcal{L}_{rec}$ and a watermark extraction loss $\\mathcal{L}_{w}$, which drives the extractor $E_{\\theta}$ to recover the message $m$ from the distilled decoder’s output:\nL136:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{dist}}=\\mathcal{L}_{rec}\\left(\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{o}}(z_{w}),\\mathcal{D}(z)\\right)+\\lambda_{w}\\mathcal{L}_{w}\\left(E_{\\theta}(\\mathcal{D}(z)),m\\right).$$  |  | (3)\nL137: where $\\mathcal{L}_{rec}=\\ell_{1}+\\lambda_{p}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LPIPS}}$ combines pixel-wise $\\ell_{1}$ loss and perceptual LPIPS loss (cite105†Zhang et al., 2018 ). Unlike Stable Signature, we train the decoder to reconstruct the watermarked image rather than just leveraging the signal from the extractor $E_{\\theta}$ to guide the distillation.\nL138: \nL139: cite106†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: The decoder of the generative model can be distilled to produce watermarked images from non-watermarked latents.\nL140: ## 5 Experiments\nL141: \nL142: We first detail our experimental setup in Section cite18†5.1 , then we present our main results on post-hoc latent watermarking (Section cite23†5.2 ) and in-model watermarking via distillation in the latent decoder (Section cite25†5.3.1 ) and in the generative model (Section cite26†5.3.2 ). Additional analysis on multi-watermarking, watermark formation, and watermark forgetting are provided in Appendices cite38†E ,  cite39†F and cite41†H .\nL143: ### 5.1 Experimental Setup\nL144: \nL145: We detail the experimental setup for our main experiments, and refer to Appendix cite31†A for additional details.\nL146: ##### Generative models.\nL147: For the diffusion model, we use the class-conditional ImageNet UViT-H model from DCAE (cite107†Chen et al., 2024b ) generating 8$\\times$8$\\times$128 latents, which are decoded into 512$\\times$512 images. For the autoregressive model, we use RAR-XL (cite73†Yu et al., 2024 ), which is based on the MaskGIT-VQGAN (cite108†Chang et al., 2022 ) autoencoder that compresses 256$\\times$256 images into 256 discrete tokens with a codebook size of 1024.\nL148: We show additional results for MaskBit (cite74†Weber et al., 2024 ) in Appendix cite40†G .\nL149: ##### Watermarking models.\nL150: For the pixel watermarking baseline, we utilize the default architecture from VideoSeal, which features a UNet embedder and a ConvNeXt-tiny extractor. For the latent watermarkers, we modify the embedder to accommodate the lower spatial resolution of the latent representations by removing all downsampling and upsampling layers while preserving the middle ResNet blocks. The extractor architecture remains unchanged across both methods.\nL151: To perform a like-for-like comparison between pixel-space and latent-space watermarking, both approaches share identical training objectives, training schedules, augmentations, and hyperparameters, with only the watermark strength $\\epsilon$ adjusted to account for the different value ranges between pixel and latent spaces. We do not use any JND masking for the pixel watermarker. We train all the models with 64-bit messages for 600k steps on ImageNet (cite109†Deng et al., 2009 ) with a batch size of 128.\nL152: For all the models, the watermark strength is set to a higher value for the first 100k steps to kickstart training, and then it is decreased to its final value over the next 100k steps with a cosine decay. For the latent watermarker of DCAE, the watermark scaling factor goes from $\\epsilon=1.5$ to $\\epsilon=0.5$.\nL153: For the latent watermarker of RAR, we consider two cases: (a) when the watermarker is applied after the quantization step, we use the initial $\\epsilon=2.0$ that is decreased to $\\epsilon=0.5$ during training; and (b) when the latent watermarker is applied before the quantization step, we use $\\epsilon=3.0$ decreased to the finial value of $\\epsilon=1.5$. For the pixel-space watermarking experiments, we use the initial $\\epsilon=0.2$ and final $\\epsilon=0.02$.\nL154: We provide additional training details in Appendix cite31†A .\nL155: Table 1: Comparing post-hoc watermarking methods on DCAE-generated images in terms of image quality and bit prediction robustness to various attacks. For post-hoc latent watermarking, we also report a coverless variant that is independent of the input.\nL156: Method  | PSNR  | FID  | IS  | Identity  | Valuemetric  | Geometric  | Compression  | Combined  | Avg\nL157: Post hoc pixel  | 43.48  | 10.84  | 99.53  | 100.00  | 99.89  | 94.70  | 99.90  | 97.29  | 97.78\nL158: Post hoc latent  | 31.06  | 11.42  | 98.11  | 99.75  | 98.08  | 91.62  | 99.23  | 84.28  | 95.18\nL159: Post hoc latent (coverless)  | 30.90  | 11.29  | 99.02  | 97.69  | 95.85  | 87.25  | 96.74  | 73.94  | 91.81\nL160: Table 2: Comparing post-hoc methods on RAR-generated images on image quality and bit prediction after various attacks. For post-hoc latent watermarking, we compare the watermarking results before and after the quantization step (’before quant’ and ‘after quant’).\nL161: Method  | PSNR  | FID  | IS  | Identity  | Valuemetric  | Geometric  | Compression  | Combined  | Avg\nL162: Post hoc pixel  | 41.80  | 3.09  | 292.75  | 100  | 99.69  | 92.97  | 99.74  | 93.93  | 97.27\nL163: Post hoc latent (before quant)  | N/A  | 3.56  | 239.00  | 96.92  | 95.91  | 87.20  | 95.98  | 77.00  | 90.60\nL164: Post hoc latent (after quant)  | 24.54  | 3.44  | 250.50  | 99.52  | 98.56  | 91.37  | 97.97  | 82.35  | 93.96\n"
